  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061z & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061z & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}{2}) \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\frac{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}{2}.$",
